WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.963
The AD CS driver.

2
00:00:01.963 --> 00:00:05.516
In the platform it's a CA
template object called Microsoft

3
00:00:05.516 --> 00:00:09.737
Active Directory Certificate
Services, Enterprise and Standalone.

4
00:00:09.737 --> 00:00:13.882
It needs Microsoft CA Support
installed on the platform servers,

5
00:00:13.882 --> 00:00:15.800
and it talks to the CA over DCOM.

6
00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.737
The account it uses needs Read,
Issue and Manage Certificates,

7
00:00:19.737 --> 00:00:21.975
and Request Certificates on the CA.

8
00:00:21.975 --> 00:00:25.925
That matches the Federal
Modernization Workbook's Book 07.

9
00:00:25.925 --> 00:00:28.300
Template mapping is done by policy.

10
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:32.638
A certificate gets its CA template
object from the policy on its folder,

11
00:00:32.638 --> 00:00:34.550
or directly on the certificate.

12
00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:38.508
CyberArk's vCert tool expects the
template to come from policy and

13
00:00:38.508 --> 00:00:42.537
calls choosing it per request
against CyberArk's recommendations.

14
00:00:42.537 --> 00:00:46.373
So, by inference, the natural
pattern is one CyberArk CA

15
00:00:46.373 --> 00:00:50.976
template object for each AD CS
template the platform issues from.

16
00:00:50.976 --> 00:00:54.200
Enterprise and standalone
CAs are both supported.

17
00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:57.732
Only enterprise CAs use
Active Directory templates,

18
00:00:57.732 --> 00:01:02.307
which is exactly why Book 07 moves
server issuance to a standalone CA

19
00:01:02.307 --> 00:01:06.962
after the forest retires, with template
policy living in the platform.

20
00:01:06.962 --> 00:01:12.375
Letting requesters set end dates
may need a CA policy flag turned on;

21
00:01:12.375 --> 00:01:16.143
that raises event 4892
under Book 07 Chapter 03,

22
00:01:16.143 --> 00:01:20.975
so the program's guidance is to
leave it off unless you need it.

23
00:01:20.975 --> 00:01:22.037
One open item.

24
00:01:22.037 --> 00:01:25.683
The driver page says it
supports CAs on all supported

25
00:01:25.683 --> 00:01:29.412
Windows Server versions, and
the platform runs on 2025.

26
00:01:29.412 --> 00:01:32.794
But CyberArk's
supported-integrations table

27
00:01:32.794 --> 00:01:36.087
lists AD CS only through
Windows Server 2022.

28
00:01:36.087 --> 00:01:42.700
If any retained CA runs Windows Server 2025,
get written confirmation before Wave 2.

29
